        "targetEvidence": {
          "articleId": "book-spine",
          "blockId": "[\"system\",2]",
          "anchor": "system",
          "quote": "A photo is first downscaled so that its long side is at most 1,600 px. A one-class oriented bounding box (OBB) detector finds the spines. Near-duplicate boxes are merged (Section 3.5), and the number of boxes scoring 0.4 or more is the book count. Each box is then cut out with a margin of 8% of its short side, rotated so that its long side is vertical, and upscaled if its short side is below 96 px.",
